## 5. Staging Database Schema {#5-staging-schema}

Add this table to your `Portal-Service` PostgreSQL database:

```sql
-- Migration: Create staged_projects table
CREATE TYPE staged_status AS ENUM (
    'pending_review',    -- Waiting for human review
    'approved',          -- Approved and pushed to production (new or updated)
    'rejected',          -- Rejected by reviewer
    'failed',            -- Push to production failed (retry-able)
    'duplicate',         -- Auto-detected or manually marked duplicate
    'update_pending'     -- Matched existing project, waiting for update review
);

CREATE TYPE staged_action AS ENUM (
    'create',            -- This is a new project, will POST to create-from-dashboard
    'update',            -- This matches an existing project, will PUT to /:id
    'skip'               -- Reviewer decided to skip (no action)
);

CREATE TABLE staged_projects (
    id                  UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),

    -- Source tracking
    source_url          TEXT NOT NULL,
    source_domain       VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,       -- 'magicbricks.com', '99acres.com', etc.
    screenshot_url      TEXT,                         -- Captured page screenshot for proof
    scraped_at          TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE DEFAULT NOW(),

    -- AI-extracted data (full payload ready for create-from-dashboard)
    extracted_data      JSONB NOT NULL,               -- The complete normalized JSON

    -- Entity resolution results
    resolved_builder_id UUID,                         -- Matched builderId (null if not found)
    resolved_city_id    UUID,                         -- Matched cityId
    resolved_zone_id    UUID,                         -- Matched zoneId (for reference only)
    resolved_location_id UUID,                        -- Matched locationId

    -- Quality metrics
    confidence_score    NUMERIC(3,2) DEFAULT 0.50,    -- 0.00 to 1.00
    validation_warnings JSONB DEFAULT '[]'::jsonb,    -- ["missing_rera", "builder_not_found"]

    -- Dedup & update tracking
    matched_project_id  UUID,                         -- Existing project this might match
    is_duplicate        BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE,
    action_type         staged_action DEFAULT 'create', -- What to do: create new or update existing
    price_change_diff   JSONB,                         -- Diff of what changed (old vs new prices/units)

    -- Review state
    status              staged_status DEFAULT 'pending_review',
    reviewed_by         UUID,                         -- Admin user who reviewed
    review_notes        TEXT,                          -- Why rejected, or what was changed
    reviewed_at         TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE,

    -- Production linkage (after approval)
    created_project_id  UUID,                         -- The projectId created in production
    pushed_at           TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE,
    push_error          TEXT,                          -- Error message if push failed

    -- Timestamps
    created_at          TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE DEFAULT NOW(),
    updated_at          TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE DEFAULT NOW()
);

-- Indexes for the review dashboard
CREATE INDEX idx_staged_status ON staged_projects(status);
CREATE INDEX idx_staged_domain ON staged_projects(source_domain);
CREATE INDEX idx_staged_confidence ON staged_projects(confidence_score DESC);
CREATE INDEX idx_staged_created ON staged_projects(created_at DESC);
```

### Why JSONB for `extracted_data`?

Because the AI extraction output maps **directly** to the `req.body` payload that your existing [`createProjectFromDashboard`](file:///c:/Users/Mark/Desktop/JustFlip/Portal-Service/controllers/portal/project.controller.js#L2506) expects. When the reviewer clicks "Approve", we literally `POST` the contents of `extracted_data` (with resolved IDs injected) to `/project/create-from-dashboard`. Zero new backend code needed for the push.

